> Added in prompt v4 from R004 evidence; v4 takes effect at the R005 run boundary, and R004 is assessed under v3. Trigger references quote the journal heading time exactly as written:
> 
> - Before opening a PR, run the repository's required regression gate (the same command as --verify) in a fresh clone checked out at the exact commit. For an unpushed commit, clone from the local repository. Record the commit, the pass, fail and skip counts, and the reason for each skip. A skip the repository does not document as needing local data is a finding, and the PR does not open until it is resolved or paused under section 17. A failure is a finding, fixed through Howl review and acceptance or paused under section 17. The controller does not hand-edit or regenerate product files to make the clone pass. It may restructure its own unpushed commits only when the final tree is unchanged, and it records this and reruns the clone gate. Any content change goes through Howl and counts against the run's session bound. The gate's check for stray ignored paths covers new output directories; a repository without such a check runs `git check-ignore` on them. A clone on this machine still shares its installed tools; the CI rule below covers that. (Trigger: 2026-10-10T16:26:38Z, 2026-10-10 20:54 UTC.)
> - The session verification command is a short command that runs the repository's required gate unchanged: an existing one such as `make test-full`, or a committed script such as `sh scripts/gate.sh`. Record the command and the content hash of every file that defines it (for a make target, the Makefile) before and after each session. A difference the independent review did not see is a finding. Not narrower means it covers at least the repository's documented required gate and its CI test step. If a session creates or edits the script, the independent review confirms it is not narrower than the required gate, and any narrowing is a finding. The --verify-timeout rule above still applies. This is intended to avoid exact-literal permission mismatches on inline shell chains; it is not known to prevent every provider's shell refusals (CUBS-P-011, provisional). (Trigger: 2026-10-10 19:49 UTC, 2026-10-10 20:39 UTC.)
> - Target every PR at the default branch. Stack a PR only when a later change cannot be built without an earlier unmerged one, and state that reason, its base and the merge order in its body. After the user merges its parent, retarget it to the default branch before the next merge. A change merged into a non-default branch is not delivered: open a PR to the default branch whose tree is identical to the verified commit. If the default branch has moved since then, rerun the gate on the PR's merge result. Record MERGED_VERIFIED only when the change is on the default branch and the gate passes on a fresh clone of it, with skip counts recorded. Merging remains under section 16 and the user's instructions; this line grants no merge authority. (Trigger: 2026-10-10 22:30 UTC; also R003 PRs #8 and #9, runs/R003/FINAL-ASSESSMENT.md.)
> - Before handing a PR to the user, read the CI checks for its head commit. Wait for pending checks for a bounded time, and record that time. A check still pending is handed off as pending, never as passed. A failure shown to come from an external outage, or present on the default branch already, is recorded as a finding with that evidence, and the PR is handed off as not verified by CI. Any other failure is fixed through Howl, and the fix session counts against the run's session bound as in the repair-session rule below; if none remains, pause under section 17. Never describe a PR with a failing check as ready. If CI is absent or unreadable, say so in the handoff. (Trigger: 2026-10-10 22:30 UTC.)
> - For user-interface work, the controller itself (not a local model) inspects screenshots at every viewport the tests cover, before recording the run outcome and preferably before Howl acceptance, so that findings can enter a goal. This adds to Howl review and acceptance and does not replace them. Record the screenshot paths, viewports and findings in the assessment. A readability problem is a finding, fixed through Howl within bounds or listed as open, and is never counted as passed. Screenshots committed to a public branch obey the third-party-data allowance: only aggregates or the examples the journaled user instruction allows. (Trigger: 2026-10-10 21:40 UTC.)
> - Before a session that would change an input the run contract (runs/<run-id>/RUN-CONTRACT.md) lists as reused or unchanged, pause under section 17 for a dated user decision, then record the amendment quoting it. Keep the original text on record and report the change as a deviation. If such a change becomes necessary during a session, stop further sessions that touch it and pause under section 17. After each such session, diff the paths the contract lists as unchanged; any change outside the approved amendment is a finding and a pause. This does not relax the v3 label-freeze rule or section 18. (Trigger: 2026-10-10T16:27:03Z, 2026-10-10 20:39 UTC, 2026-10-10 21:40 UTC.)
> - A HowlPlane session that repairs a Howl defect counts against the run's session bound unless a dated user instruction exempts it. Record which applies in the session's INTENT journal entry, quoting the instruction if it is exempt. (Trigger: 2026-10-10 18:28 UTC and 2026-10-10 22:30 UTC: R004 ran repair sessions 03d1d55e and d516f2a9, recorded as not product sessions, beyond its six-session bound.)
